# Sidecar L4DEL annotations for openai/math (Lean sources under lean/). Paths are relative to the lean root.
# The ComparatorChallenges files state a result with a `sorry` body on purpose: the matching .json names
# the solution module (under OAI/) that the Comparator tool checks against the same statement.

@file ComparatorChallenges/QuasiRiemannHypothesis.lean
@decl OAI.riemannZeta_ne_zero_of_seven_eighths_lt_re
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Zero-free half-plane for the Riemann zeta function
-- L4DEL: intuition: The Riemann zeta function has no zeros anywhere to the right of the vertical line with real part 7/8.
-- L4DEL: step: The statement takes a complex s with real part strictly greater than 7/8 and concludes riemannZeta s is not zero.
-- L4DEL: step: In this challenge file the proof body is sorry; the paired JSON file names the solution module that the Comparator tool checks against this statement.
-- L4DEL: term: Comparator challenge = a file that states a theorem with a sorry body so the Comparator tool can check that a separate solution module proves exactly that statement

@file ComparatorChallenges/DirichletSevenEighths.lean
@decl OAI.DirichletCharacter.LFunction_ne_zero_of_seven_eighths_lt_re
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Zero-free half-plane for Dirichlet L-functions
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every Dirichlet L-function, for every modulus and character, is nonzero to the right of real part 7/8, apart from the pole of the trivial character at 1.
-- L4DEL: step: The hypotheses are a modulus q, a Dirichlet character chi, a point s with real part above 7/8, and the exclusion of the case chi = 1 and s = 1.
-- L4DEL: step: The conclusion is chi.LFunction s is not zero; this challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: term: Dirichlet L-function = the analytic continuation of the Dirichlet series sum chi(n) n^(-s) attached to a Dirichlet character chi

@file ComparatorChallenges/SiegelZeros.lean
@decl OAI.SiegelZeros.WeightedTorusJets.exists_absolute_real_zero_gap
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Uniform gap for real zeros of L-functions
-- L4DEL: intuition: There is one positive constant c such that any real zero beta of a primitive real Dirichlet L-function of conductor q satisfies 1 - beta at least c / log q.
-- L4DEL: step: The statement obtains a single c greater than 0, with no explicit value given.
-- L4DEL: step: For every q at least 3, every primitive nonprincipal character chi taking real values, and every real zero beta strictly between 0 and 1, it concludes c <= (1 - beta) * log q.
-- L4DEL: step: This bounds real zeros away from 1, in the shape of a Landau-Siegel zero exclusion; the challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: term: Landau-Siegel zero = a hypothetical real zero of a Dirichlet L-function very close to 1

@file ComparatorChallenges/HadwigerCounterexample.lean
@decl OAI.HadwigerCounterexample.not_hadwiger_conjecture
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Hadwiger's conjecture fails
-- L4DEL: intuition: The statement denies that every finite graph's chromatic number is at most its largest clique-minor size.
-- L4DEL: step: Graphs are simple graphs on Fin m, and hadwigerNumber is the supremum of t such that the graph has a clique minor of order t (connected disjoint branch sets, pairwise touching).
-- L4DEL: step: The statement is the negation of "for all m and all graphs G on Fin m, chromaticNumber G is at most hadwigerNumber G".
-- L4DEL: step: This challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: term: clique minor = a family of disjoint connected vertex sets in a graph, every two of which have an edge between them

@decl OAI.HadwigerCounterexample.exists_counterexamples
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Counterexamples of arbitrarily large order
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every bound M there is a graph on m >= max(M, 5) vertices with independence number at most 2 whose chromatic number is above its Hadwiger number.
-- L4DEL: step: The graph G on Fin m has independence number at most 2.
-- L4DEL: step: Its connected matching number is below m/100 and its Hadwiger number is below 26m/75 + 2/3, which is itself below m/2.
-- L4DEL: step: The chromatic number is finite, is at least m/2, and exceeds the Hadwiger number; the challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.

@file ComparatorChallenges/KaplanskyDirectFiniteness.lean
@decl OAI.KaplanskyCounterexample.MainClaim
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Failure of direct finiteness in characteristic 2
-- L4DEL: intuition: A group algebra over a finite field of characteristic 2, of a finitely generated group, can contain elements a, b with a*b = 1 but b*a different from 1.
-- L4DEL: step: The claim has the form: there are a finite field K with CharP K 2, a finitely generated group G, and a, b in MonoidAlgebra K G.
-- L4DEL: step: Those satisfy a * b = 1 and b * a != 1.

@decl OAI.KaplanskyCounterexample.main_theorem
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Main theorem of the Kaplansky counterexample
-- L4DEL: intuition: States that MainClaim holds; the challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: step: The theorem is just MainClaim, a statement about one-sided inverses in a group algebra.
-- L4DEL: prereq: OAI.KaplanskyCounterexample.MainClaim@ComparatorChallenges/KaplanskyDirectFiniteness.lean

@file ComparatorChallenges/MahlerConjecture.lean
@decl OAI.SymmetricMahler.symmetric_mahler
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Symmetric Mahler volume bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: For a centrally symmetric convex body K in n dimensions, volume(K) times volume(its polar) is at least 4^n / n!.
-- L4DEL: step: K is compact, convex, symmetric under x to -x, with nonempty interior, in Fin n to R with n at least 1.
-- L4DEL: step: coordinatePolar K is the set of p with the sum of p_i v_i at most 1 for all v in K.
-- L4DEL: step: The conclusion compares the product of the two Lebesgue volumes (taken as real numbers) with 4^n / n!; the challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: term: polar body = the set of vectors p whose pairing with every point of K is at most 1

@file ComparatorChallenges/AffineBernstein.lean
@decl OAI.AffineBernstein.affine_bernstein
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Affine Bernstein theorem in dimensions 3 to 9
-- L4DEL: intuition: A complete, smooth, uniformly convex solution of the affine maximal surface equation on a convex domain must be a paraboloid.
-- L4DEL: step: The hypotheses fix n between 3 and 9, an open convex nonempty domain, smooth u with positive definite Hessian, the affine maximal equation holding, and completeness for the graph's Euclidean path metric.
-- L4DEL: step: The conclusion has three parts: the domain is the whole space, u is a quadratic polynomial with symmetric positive definite matrix, and an affine equivalence carries the standard paraboloid onto the graph of u.
-- L4DEL: step: The challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: prereq: OAI.AffineBernstein.AffineMaximalOn@ComparatorChallenges/AffineBernstein.lean, OAI.AffineBernstein.EuclideanGraphComplete@ComparatorChallenges/AffineBernstein.lean
-- L4DEL: term: affine maximal = satisfying the fourth-order equation that the cofactor-Hessian contracted with the Hessian of det(D2u)^(-(n+1)/(n+2)) vanishes
